    def help_action(self) -> None:
        heading("Git in plain English")
        print(
            textwrap.dedent(
                """\
                Repository      The .git folder that stores history and settings.
                Working tree    The files you see and edit.
                Staging area    A waiting room: git add puts changes here.
                Commit          A saved snapshot of the staging area.
                Branch          A movable name pointing at a line of commits.
                Remote          A copy of the repository elsewhere, usually origin on GitHub.
                Upstream        The remote branch your current branch tracks.
                Fetch           Downloads remote information without changing your files.
                Pull            Fetches and moves your branch forward; --ff-only forbids a merge commit.
                Push            Uploads your local commits to the remote branch.

                working tree --git add--> staging area --git commit--> local repo --git push--> remote

                A commit records the staging area, not automatically every file in the
                folder. That is why the app shows what is staged, scans it for secrets,
                and asks before committing.

                If local and remote both have commits the other lacks, the histories are
                diverged. That is not a reason to force-push. It is a reason to compare
                the graph, keep a backup, and merge or rebase deliberately.
                """
            )
        )
        heading("What the commands do and why")
        print(
            textwrap.dedent(
                """\
                git config --global       Read/write your personal Git settings.
                                          Why: identity, safer defaults, and SSH signing
                                          should be set once for every repository.

                git init -b main          Create a new .git repository on branch main.
                                          Why: Git needs a repository before it can track
                                          or commit anything.

                git status                Show changed, staged, and untracked files.
                                          Why: review reality before acting.

                git add                   Put selected changes into the staging area.
                                          Why: choose exactly what the next commit contains.

                git diff --cached        Show what is staged for the next commit.
                                          Why: verify the snapshot before saving it.

                git commit                Save the staged snapshot as a signed commit.
                                          Why: create a reviewable checkpoint; nothing is
                                          uploaded yet.

                git fetch                 Download remote refs without changing your branch.
                                          Why: compare local and remote history safely.

                git pull --ff-only        Fast-forward only; refuse to create a merge commit.
                                          Why: update a branch without hiding divergence.

                git push                  Upload local commits to the remote branch.
                                          Why: share history with GitHub. The app never forces.

                git log / git branch      Inspect history, branches, and backup points.
                                          Why: understand the current state before changing it.

                git merge-base            Find the common ancestor of two histories.
                                          Why: distinguish unrelated repositories from
                                          histories that merely diverged.

                git fsck                  Check object-database integrity.
                                          Why: detect missing or corrupt repository objects.

                git switch / branch       List, create, switch, rename, or delete branches.
                                          Why: isolate work and control which history grows.

                git reset --soft          Remove the last local commit but keep its changes.
                                          Why: undo an unpublished commit without losing work.
                                          The app refuses if HEAD is already on a remote.

                git revert                Create a new commit that reverses an old commit.
                                          Why: safely undo shared history without force-pushing.

                git stash                 Save, list, apply, or drop uncommitted changes.
                                          Why: move between tasks without making a commit.

                git merge / rebase        Integrate or replay branch histories.
                                          Why: merge preserves shared history; rebase is
                                          restricted to local-only commits.

                git tag / gh release      Mark commits and publish GitHub releases.
                                          Why: version and distribute checkpoints.

                gh repo create            Create a GitHub repository and optionally push.
                gh repo clone             Clone a GitHub repository using gh authentication.
                gh repo rename            Rename the GitHub repository and update origin.
                gh auth login/refresh     Authenticate gh or add OAuth scopes.
                gh api user/ssh_signing_keys
                                          Read the signing keys registered on GitHub.
                """
            )
        )
        heading("Why the app checks things")
        print(
            textwrap.dedent(
                """\
                Identity        Commits with a missing or wrong author are hard to fix.
                Default branch  A consistent main branch avoids main/master confusion.
                pull.ff only    Prevents an accidental merge commit during a pull.
                SSH signing     Proves a commit came from your key and was not changed.
                allowed_signers Lets Git verify SSH signatures locally.
                URL rewrite     Makes HTTPS GitHub URLs use your SSH identity.
                .gitignore      Keeps secrets and build output out of Git history.
                .dockerignore   Keeps secrets and .git out of Docker build contexts.
                Remote check    Confirms fetch/push targets and detects divergence.
                Secret scan     Catches common private-key and token patterns before commit.
                """
            )
        )
        heading("Scenario guide")
        print(
            textwrap.dedent(
                """\
                A) New private GitHub repository, then clone it
                   Best when you have no code yet and want a safe starting point.
                   gh repo create NAME --private --add-readme --clone

                B) Clone a repository that already exists
                   gh repo clone OWNER/REPO
                   or: git clone git@github.com:OWNER/REPO.git

                C) Existing local project becomes a new GitHub repository
                   Creates .gitignore first, initializes main, reviews/stages files,
                   signs the initial commit, then creates the remote and pushes.
                   If the folder is already a Git repository with no remote, option
                   6 publishes its existing history or connects an existing URL.

                D) Local and remote repositories both exist
                   Fetches, shows graph/branches/merge-base, creates an optional local
                   backup branch, and never force-pushes.

                Rename (option 9)
                   Renames the local folder and/or the GitHub repository. GitHub
                   redirects the old name, and the local origin URL is repaired if
                   needed. A rename does not require a commit.

                Commit and push (option 8)
                   Shows changes, stages all or selected paths, scans staged files
                   for secrets, commits with SSH signing, then performs a normal
